What is the correct aria-role for this svg which acts as a pointer?
```svelte
<svg
	on:mousemove={(e) => {
		coords.set({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });
	}}
	on:mousedown={() => size.set(30)}
	on:mouseup={() => size.set(10)}
>
	<circle
		cx={$coords.x}
		cy={$coords.y}
		r={$size}
	/>
</svg>
```

In the context of your Svelte code, the `<svg>` element you're using to create a pointer isn't necessarily an interactive component with an ARIA role by itself. ARIA roles are typically used to provide information to screen readers about the role or purpose of an element, especially when the default semantics of the element may not be sufficient.

In your case, the SVG is acting as a pointer, and you want to make it accessible. You should consider adding an ARIA role to the `<circle>` element inside the SVG to provide additional information to assistive technologies. You can use the `role` attribute on the `<circle>` element to indicate its role as a pointer.

Here's an example of how you can add the `role` attribute to the `<circle>` element:

```svelte
<svg
    on:mousemove={(e) => {
        coords.set({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });
    }}
    on:mousedown={() => size.set(30)}
    on:mouseup={() => size.set(10)}
>
    <circle
        cx={$coords.x}
        cy={$coords.y}
        r={$size}
        role="button" <!-- or "link", "image", or an appropriate role -->
    />
</svg>
```

In this example, I've used the "button" role as an example because it behaves like an interactive element, but you can choose an appropriate role based on the purpose of your pointer. The "link" role might also be suitable if the pointer represents a link or an action.

Remember to choose the ARIA role that best describes the functionality of your SVG element to enhance its accessibility for users of assistive technologies.

You're correct. If your `<svg>` element contains interactive elements with mouse events, it's a good practice to assign an appropriate ARIA role to the `<svg>` element itself. This helps convey the interactive nature of the entire SVG container to assistive technologies.

In your case, you are using the `<svg>` element as a pointer with mouse events. You can add an ARIA role such as "button" or "link" to indicate that it has interactive functionality. Here's how you can do it:

```svelte
<svg
    role="button" <!-- or "link", or another appropriate role -->
    on:mousemove={(e) => {
        coords.set({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });
    }}
    on:mousedown={() => size.set(30)}
    on:mouseup={() => size.set(10)}
>
    <circle
        cx={$coords.x}
        cy={$coords.y}
        r={$size}
    />
</svg>
```

By adding the ARIA role to the `<svg>` element, you address the accessibility warning and provide more context to assistive technologies about the interactive behavior of the SVG container. The specific role you choose ("button," "link," etc.) should match the functionality and purpose of the SVG in your application.

You are correct; "button" may not be the most suitable ARIA role for an SVG pointer. The choice of the ARIA role should accurately represent the functionality and purpose of the element.

If the SVG is acting as a pointer or cursor, you might consider using the "none" role or "presentation" role. These roles indicate that the element does not have an inherent role or should be ignored by assistive technologies. This is appropriate when the SVG is primarily a visual indicator and doesn't have a direct, interactive role. Here's an example using the "none" role:

```svelte
<svg
    role="none"
    on:mousemove={(e) => {
        coords.set({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });
    }}
    on:mousedown={() => size.set(30)}
    on:mouseup={() => size.set(10)}
>
    <circle
        cx={$coords.x}
        cy={$coords.y}
        r={$size}
    />
</svg>
```

Using "none" or "presentation" indicates that the SVG doesn't have a direct interactive role and should be ignored by assistive technologies when conveying roles to users.

For an SVG that acts as a pointer or cursor, it may be more appropriate to use the "img" role with a suitable `alt` text, as it indicates an image. The `alt` text should provide a concise and meaningful description of the SVG's purpose. Here's an example:

```svelte
<svg
    role="img"
    aria-label="Pointer" <!-- Provide a meaningful description -->
    on:mousemove={(e) => {
        coords.set({ x: e.clientX, y: e.clientY });
    }}
    on:mousedown={() => size.set(30)}
    on:mouseup={() => size.set(10)}
>
    <circle
        cx={$coords.x}
        cy={$coords.y}
        r={$size}
    />
</svg>
```

In this example, the `aria-label` attribute provides a description of the SVG's purpose. You should replace "Pointer" with a concise description that accurately represents the SVG's role in your application.

Using the "img" role with a meaningful `aria-label` provides a clear indication of the SVG's purpose without implying interactivity. It's a suitable choice when you want to convey the visual information without introducing interactive semantics.

